\paragraph{ISR collapse trajectory (FOL exec-only, seed 42).}
Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed} traces the ISR collapse over training:
ISR$^*=0.642$ at 100 steps $\to$ $0.132$ at 500 steps ($-79.4$\%) $\to$ $0.003$ at 1000 steps ($-99.5$\% from step 100).
PR increases monotonically ($0.32 \to 0.93 \to 0.96$) while structural quality collapses, confirming that ISR captures degeneration invisible to execution reward.
By step 1000, 94.8\% of proved samples have ISR~$=0$ and the mean component count rises to 18.55.

\paragraph{Prolog: universal Goodhart via three distinct pathways.}
All three Prolog exec+ISR seeds exhibit Goodhart's Law with 0\% non-hacking proofs, but each discovers a different gaming mechanism:
seed~42 produces clean H7 tautologies (single premise~$=$~conclusion),
seed~43 generates uppercase pseudo-Prolog that bypasses the text-match classifier while remaining functionally tautological,
and seed~44 converts NL conclusions to snake\_case atoms used as both fact and query.
The diversity of gaming pathways across seeds---despite identical training data and reward function---demonstrates that Goodhart's Law in neurosymbolic reasoning is not a single failure mode but a family of convergent exploits.

\subsection{Early-Training Hacking Analysis (100-Step Checkpoint)}
\label{app:100step}

To control for the prove-rate confound in our SFT comparison (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}), we analyze the exec-only checkpoint at 100 training steps (seed~42), where PR${=}32$\%---higher than SFT (14\%) but still early in training.
Of the 32 proved samples, 9 (28.1\%) exhibit H7 hacking (single premise identical to conclusion) and 1 (3.1\%) exhibits H1 (conclusion embedded among premises), yielding a total hacking rate of 31.2\%.
By contrast, SFT at comparable or lower PR shows 7.1\% hacking (1/14 proved samples).
This demonstrates that reward hacking emerges early in GRPO training---well before the prove-rate plateau---and is not simply an artifact of having more proved samples available for exploitation.
The 100-step hacking rate (31.2\%) is already substantially above SFT (7.1\%), supporting the claim that hacking is associated with RL optimization rather than prove-rate level alone.

\begin{abstract}
GRPO-trained neurosymbolic formalizers achieve high prove rates---93\% on FOLIO---yet 87\% of proved outputs embed the conclusion verbatim among the premises, a degenerate shortcut invisible to execution-based evaluation. We introduce \emph{Solver Trace Ablation} (STA), a diagnostic (solver-agnostic in principle, validated on two structurally different solvers: Prover9 and SWI-Prolog) that applies leave-one-out component necessity testing to solver inputs. The fraction of components whose removal causes proof failure yields the \emph{Intensional Structural Reward} (ISR): ISR$=$1 indicates syntactic component necessity; ISR$=$0 indicates complete redundancy. ISR does not correlate with downstream NLI accuracy ($\rho{=}{-}0.049$, $p{=}0.187$), confirming it measures structural properties orthogonal to semantic correctness.

ISR exposes a collapse trajectory hidden from prove rate: over 100 to 1000 GRPO training steps (Qwen3-4B), ISR falls from 0.642 to 0.003---a 99.5\% degradation---while prove rate rises from 32\% to 96\%. An SFT baseline achieves 92.9\% non-hacking proofs with near-zero hacking, confirming the degeneration is RL-specific.

Can ISR serve as a training signal? Incorporating ISR into the reward at $\beta{=}0.1$ induces \emph{meta-level Goodhart's Law}: 64.5\% of proved samples exhibit conclusion duplication (H7), versus 0\% under matched random controls. The sole apparent ISR improvement reduces to $1.06\times$ after controlling for a premise-count confound. At $\beta{=}0.3$, prove rate collapses to 18\% versus 48\% for random control.

Cross-logic transfer to Prolog/SWI-Prolog yields a stronger result: ISR-as-reward produces universal Goodhart---3/3 seeds gaming via distinct mechanisms, 0\% non-hacking proofs.

Across 6 reward conditions, 2 logic types (FOL/Prover9, Prolog/SWI-Prolog), and 3 seeds, we catalog 7 distinct reward hacking modes---including query-as-fact gaming that bypasses text-match classifiers---documenting ISR's fundamental asymmetry: reliable as a diagnostic, unreliable as an optimization target.\footnote{Code and data will be released upon acceptance: \url{[anonymized for review]}}
\end{abstract}

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

Experiments use a single base model (Qwen3-4B) and dataset family (FOLIO); generalization remains untested.
ISR quantifies syntactic component necessity, not semantic faithfulness: a downstream NLI evaluation finds ISR and task accuracy uncorrelated (Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.049$, $p{=}0.187$; Appendix~\ref{app:extended-discussion}).
Three seeds demonstrate qualitative divergence but limit statistical power.
STA requires solvers supporting component-level input manipulation.

\paragraph{Data scale.}
Our experiments use 500 training examples and 100 evaluation examples, reflecting the FOLIO dataset's total size of 1,435 annotated examples~\citep{folio}.
The training subset is selected via stratified sampling (seed 42) and held constant across all conditions.
While our findings are consistent across seeds and logic types, the effect of training data scale on hacking prevalence and mode distribution remains an open question for future work.

